# O'Pip Feature Bus SHADOW evidence capture (R4-B2 shadow cadence bridge)
# Dedicated, bounded, non-overlapping evidence producer for the R4-B2 shadow
# comparator path. This is NOT the protected unified cycle: it runs as its own
# process on the one scheduler (cron) and never takes the unified-cycle lock, so
# it cannot delay or skip a protection cycle.
#
# Cadence: every minute. The frozen F3 IGNITION detector requires a 60-second
# evaluation grid, evaluation_time == snapshot.evaluation_cutoff, and two
# CONSECUTIVE 60-second qualifying evaluations. A slower cadence materializes
# snapshots at non-adjacent cutoffs, so F3 could never reach persistence. One
# pass = one closed minute = one genuine FeatureSnapshot per target instrument.
#
# Capture is authorized only when OPIP_FEATURE_BUS_MODE=shadow AND
# OPIP_CANONICAL_WRITER_MODE=shadow; otherwise it is an inert recorded no-op.
#
# Non-overlap is guaranteed at TWO levels, and the ENFORCEABLE one is INSIDE the
# container:
#   * The capture process itself holds a process-level advisory lock
#     (OPIP_FEATURE_BUS_CAPTURE_LOCK, default /tmp/opip-feature-bus-capture.lock)
#     for the whole pass. This directly guarantees the invariant "at most one
#     capture_feature_bus_shadow process executes inside the container at any
#     instant", even if the host Docker client dies independently and leaves a
#     surviving in-container process. A refused invocation records an explicit
#     SKIPPED_LOCK_HELD disposition rather than silently exiting.
#   * The host `flock -n` remains as a cheap first-line guard. It is NOT the
#     enforceable guard: killing the host Docker client does not reliably
#     terminate the process created inside the container.
#
# Timeout: the enforceable wall-clock bound runs INSIDE the container
# (`timeout --signal=TERM --kill-after=5s 50 python -m ...`), directly
# supervising the Python process so it is genuinely terminated. A host-side
# `timeout` around `docker compose exec` would release the host lock while the
# in-container process could continue, so NO outer timeout wraps the call; the
# in-container process lock prevents overlap regardless.
#
# Bounds (derived, not arbitrary; the whole pass must finish inside its minute):
#   * per-instrument request: exactly ONE bounded public Kraken OHLC request (no
#     bulk multi-pair endpoint exists). The client's per-ATTEMPT timeout is
#     DERIVED so the transport's WHOLE retry budget still fits one 15s wave
#     (15s / (KRAKEN_PUBLIC_MAX_RETRIES + 1); currently 5s per attempt). See
#     app/services/opip_feature_bus_market_source.capture_request_timeout_seconds.
#     Without that derivation a single stalled request could consume 3 * 15s = 45s
#     -- the entire pass budget -- and this producer would be killed by the bound
#     below before it could record any disposition (a silent evidence drop).
#   * bounded acquisition concurrency: OPIP_FEATURE_BUS_CAPTURE_CONCURRENCY
#     (default 4, max 8). Worst case = ceil(N / concurrency) bounded requests, so
#     8 instruments <= 2 * 15s = 30s, well inside the minute. The shared Kraken
#     transport is thread-safe and rate-limited (token bucket).
#   * internal wall-clock budget: OPIP_FEATURE_BUS_CAPTURE_BUDGET_SECONDS
#     (default 45; capped at 50). The internal budget is the graceful stop.
#   * enforceable termination: in-container `timeout ... 50` with kill-after 5s,
#     so the Python process is dead by ~55s < the 60s slot.
#   * protection (the 60s unified cycle) is a separate process on the same
#     scheduler and is never contended by this pass.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

* * * * * root flock -n /var/run/opip-feature-bus-capture.lock -c 'cd /opt/OHM-Trade-Agent-v1/OHM-Trade-Agent-v1 && /usr/bin/docker compose exec -T ohm-trade-agent timeout --signal=TERM --kill-after=5s 50 python -m app.jobs.capture_feature_bus_shadow >> /var/log/opip-feature-bus-capture.log 2>&1'
